        <p class="lede">
          Most runners do best with about 1.2 to 2.0 g of protein per kilogram of body weight a day, the range set by the American College of Sports Medicine and in line with the International Society of Sports Nutrition, spread across meals, with roughly 0.3 g/kg (about 20 to 25 g for most people) after hard or long sessions. The single biggest difference between a runner's shake and a lifter's shake is carbohydrate: after a long run or before a second session the same day, refuelling glycogen matters as much as repairing muscle. For that job our top pick is a carb-plus-protein mix such as Tailwind Recovery Mix; for everyday protein, an easy-digesting, NSF Certified for Sport whey isolate such as Klean Isolate or Thorne. Below are the numbers, the carb-to-protein ratios that research supports, how to keep shakes gentle on a runner's gut, and eight picks with a comparison table.
        </p>

