            Runners do not need a special protein powder, but they do need a slightly different strategy from gym-goers. Their protein needs are higher than the sedentary RDA of 0.8 g/kg, their training depletes glycogen in a way strength work does not, and their stomachs have to cope with shakes close to hard efforts. The best protein powder for runners is therefore one that is easy to digest, pairs well with carbohydrate, and, for anyone who races in drug-tested events, carries an independent certification.
          </p>

          <p>
            For long runs, workouts and two-a-day training, pick a carbohydrate-plus-protein recovery mix or make one by adding protein powder to a carbohydrate source. For everyday protein on easy days, a plain whey isolate or certified plant protein covers it. And for competitive runners, filter by the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport registries first and taste second.
          </p>

