            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Where you sit in that range depends on training load and goals. A recreational runner logging 20 miles a week can sit near the bottom. A marathoner in a high-mileage block, a runner trying to lose weight while keeping muscle, or a masters runner over 50 should aim higher. Our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">protein needs guide</a> and <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> will give you a personal number.
          </p>

          <p>
            Distribution matters about as much as the total. Both position stands suggest spreading protein across the day in doses of roughly 0.25 to 0.3 g/kg every three to five hours, which for most runners means 20 to 30 g at each of three or four meals. A shake is useful when it fills a gap in that pattern, most often straight after a run when appetite is low, or as a bedtime snack. It is not useful as a reason to skip breakfast.
          </p>

          <p>
            Is more better? Not meaningfully. Above about 1.6 g/kg, studies on resistance-trained adults show gains in muscle flatten out, and there is no evidence that a runner eating 2.5 g/kg recovers faster than one eating 1.8 g/kg. Extra protein is not harmful for healthy kidneys, but it takes up appetite that carbohydrate, the fuel running actually burns, needs. For most runners the bottleneck is carbohydrate, not protein.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">FOOD FIRST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Most runners can hit their number without powder</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A 70 kg runner aiming for 1.6 g/kg needs about 112 g a day. Greek yogurt at breakfast, a chicken or bean lunch, a dinner with fish or tofu and a glass of milk gets there. A shake earns its place when training, travel or appetite makes that hard.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

