        <div id="carb-protein-recovery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is the Best Carb-to-Protein Ratio for a Runner's Recovery Shake?
          </h2>

          <p>
            After a long run or a hard workout, a runner's muscles are low on glycogen as well as in need of repair. When the next session is less than about eight hours away, the ACSM position statement recommends 1.0 to 1.2 g of carbohydrate per kilogram per hour for the first four hours to refuel quickly. Protein comes alongside: around 0.3 g/kg supports muscle repair and, when carbohydrate intake falls short of that target, adding protein appears to help glycogen restoration.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is where the familiar 3:1 and 4:1 carbohydrate-to-protein ratios come from. Low-fat chocolate milk happens to land in that zone, and small studies in trained cyclists, such as Karp and colleagues in 2006, found it performed as well as or better than commercial recovery drinks for a subsequent endurance effort. The ratio itself is not magic. What matters is getting enough carbohydrate to refuel and enough protein to repair, and a 3:1 or 4:1 mix is a convenient way to hit both in one drink.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plain protein powder has almost no carbohydrate, so on its own it is not a recovery drink in this sense. A 70 kg runner who drinks a 21 g whey shake after a two-hour long run has covered protein but taken in maybe 3 g of the 70 to 85 g of carbohydrate an hour that rapid refuelling calls for. Either choose a mix designed for this, such as Tailwind Recovery Mix, or build one: a scoop of whey isolate blended with a banana, a cup of juice or milk and a handful of oats gets you close to a 3:1 drink with about 25 g of protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your next run is a day away, the urgency fades. Ordinary meals with carbohydrate and protein will refill glycogen within 24 hours for most runners, and the exact composition of the first snack matters much less. That is why we suggest a recovery mix for long runs and double days, and plain protein, or no supplement at all, after easy miles. Our guide to <a href="/when-to-take-protein-powder/">when to take protein powder</a> covers the timing evidence in more depth.
          </p>

