        <div id="easy-digestion" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Protein Powder Is Easiest on a Runner's Stomach?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gastrointestinal trouble is one of the most common complaints in endurance sport, and running, with its jostling and reduced blood flow to the gut during hard efforts, makes it worse. The protein powder that suits a lifter perfectly can leave a runner sprinting for a portable toilet at mile four. A few label choices reduce that risk considerably.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose isolate over concentrate if dairy bothers you. Whey isolate is filtered to remove most of the lactose and fat, and hydrolyzed isolates such as Dymatize ISO100 are partly pre-digested. For runners who are lactose-sensitive, isolate is usually enough; for those who react to milk protein itself, a certified plant protein is the alternative, though some runners find pea-based blends gassy. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-sensitive-stomach/">sensitive-stomach guide</a> and <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">whey isolate guide</a> go deeper.
          </p>

          <p>
            Avoid sugar alcohols and heavy gums close to a run. Erythritol, sorbitol, maltitol and xylitol draw water into the gut and are a classic cause of runner's trots. Inulin, chicory root fiber and large doses of added fiber can do the same. Many runners do fine with these at other times of day; the rule is simply not to drink them in the hours before a workout or race. Unflavored or lightly sweetened powders such as Klean Isolate sidestep the problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep fat and fiber low before running and save the bigger shakes for afterwards. If you take protein before a run at all, a small, thin isolate shake an hour or more beforehand is usually tolerated better than a thick smoothie with nut butter and seeds. And as with gels, never try a new product on race day: test it after training runs first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tested-products" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Do Competitive Runners Need Third-Party Tested Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you race in events that drug test, which includes national championships, many elite and age-group fields and collegiate competition, you are responsible for everything in your body under the strict liability principle of the World Anti-Doping Code. Supplements are a known route to accidental positives, because some products have been found to contain stimulants, steroids or other banned substances not listed on the label. US Anti-Doping (USADA) advises athletes who choose to use supplements to pick products certified by a third-party program that tests for banned substances.
          </p>

          <p>
            The two programs most relevant to protein powder are NSF Certified for Sport and Informed Sport. Both screen products for banned substances; NSF also verifies the label contents and audits manufacturing. Informed Choice, a sister program to Informed Sport, uses retail sampling and is common on US powders too. None of them can guarantee a product is completely risk-free, but they reduce the risk substantially. Our dedicated guide to <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">NSF certified protein powder</a> explains the differences and shows how to look up a product in the registries.
          </p>

          <p>
            Even if you never get tested, certification has value. The same screening that looks for banned substances also catches products that do not contain what they claim, and NSF's program includes contaminant screening. For a runner putting a scoop of something into their body every day for years, that is a reasonable filter to apply. Five of the eight picks above were listed in the NSF Certified for Sport registry when we checked on November 8, 2026.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the exact product and flavor. Certification is granted product by product, and sometimes flavor by flavor, so a brand having one certified product does not mean every tub carries the mark. Look for the seal on the label and confirm it in the registry before relying on it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="runner-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Protein for Different Kinds of Runners
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Marathon and ultra runners</h3>

          <p>
            High-mileage training raises protein needs toward the top of the 1.2 to 2.0 g/kg range, and long runs make carbohydrate recovery a weekly priority. A recovery mix after the long run, a plain protein shake when meals fall short, and casein or Greek yogurt before bed during peak weeks is a reasonable pattern. Research on pre-sleep protein, such as studies by Res and colleagues, has shown that around 40 g of casein before sleep increases overnight muscle protein synthesis; whether that translates into better running is less clear, but it is an easy way to hit a daily target. See our <a href="/best-casein-protein-powder/">casein guide</a> for options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Female runners and energy availability</h3>

          <p>
            Low energy availability, eating too little to cover training, is common in runners and underlies Relative Energy Deficiency in Sport (RED-S), which the International Olympic Committee has addressed in a series of consensus statements. It can affect bone health, hormones, immunity and performance. A protein shake does not fix an energy shortfall on its own, and a low-calorie isolate can even make it easier to feel full while under-eating. If periods have become irregular or stopped, or stress fractures keep happening, see a sports medicine doctor or a sports dietitian.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Masters runners</h3>

          <p>
            Older muscles respond less to a given dose of protein, a phenomenon often called anabolic resistance. Researchers commonly suggest that adults over about 50 aim for the higher end of daily targets and around 0.4 g/kg per meal, which for a 70 kg runner is about 28 g. A full scoop of whey isolate after runs and at protein-light meals is a practical way to get there.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Runners who lift</h3>

          <p>
            Most coaches now recommend that distance runners do two short strength sessions a week, because stronger hips, calves and feet are linked with better running economy and may help with injury resilience. If you have added lifting to your plan, protein needs move toward the middle or top of the range, and a shake after a combined strength-and-run day is a sensible use of a scoop. You do not need a mass-building approach; our guide to <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-muscle-gain/">protein powder for muscle gain</a> explains when more protein actually changes results and when it does not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Vegan runners</h3>

          <p>
            Plant proteins tend to have less leucine and lower digestibility than whey, so plant-based runners generally benefit from slightly larger servings, around 25 to 30 g, and from blends that combine sources such as pea and rice. Vega Sport is the certified pick here; our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">plant-based protein guide</a> covers more.
          </p>
        </div>

