          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a protein shake as a recovery drink</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Plain protein powder has almost no carbohydrate. After a long run or before a second session, glycogen refuelling matters as much as muscle repair. Add a banana, juice, oats or milk to your shake, or use a mix that already contains carbohydrate, rather than relying on protein alone.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Drinking sugar alcohols before a run</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Erythritol, sorbitol and similar sweeteners draw water into the gut and are a common trigger for runner's diarrhea. If a powder contains them, keep it for after training. Before running, choose a small, thin, unflavored or lightly sweetened isolate, or skip protein altogether.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the brand is certified</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                NSF and Informed Sport certify specific products, sometimes specific flavors. One certified tub from a brand does not cover its whole range. If you race in drug-tested events, find the exact product and flavor in the registry before buying, and keep a record of the lot number.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Under-eating and calling it lean</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Runners chasing a race weight sometimes swap meals for low-calorie protein shakes. That can push energy availability too low, raising the risk of RED-S, stress fractures and stalled performance. A shake should add to meals, not replace them, during heavy training.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trying a new product on race day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Race morning is the worst time to discover a powder disagrees with you. Test any new shake or recovery mix after several training runs, at the same time of day and in the same amounts you plan to use when it counts.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

